Michał Bieliński <dungeon_keeper@tlen.pl> wrote:
> Hello, NetHackers!
>
> Recently I have been wondering what rules govern when NetHack asks
> you to name an item and when the thing just gets identified outright.

I don't think you will find a behavior that is consistent across the
whole game.

> I am trying to see a pattern that would tell under which circumstances
> to reveal identity of used item to the player.  Knowing nothing about
> the new item to be added can you name general rules when to ask player
> to call it, when to auto-identify the item and when to not do anything?
>
> Dropping rings in a sink produces a message that gives it away but the
> game asks for a name.  Presumably because the player is not considered
> spoiled.

Probably and because the item is gone, you can't name it anymore.

In UnNetHack and other forks, if you get the message, the ring is
automatically identified for you. Because it is just tedious to look
it up in source or on nethackwiki. 

> A monster chucking potions at you may cause you to get knowledge of
> their type.  Potions of confusion and booze have the same effect so
> these do not get identified.

Not all other potions do this.

A potion of hallucination doesn't get identified, even though it has a
unique message. It's not often apparent why some actions lead to
identification.

For example a thrown potion of hallucination at you. This behavior was
added in NetHack 1.3d. Finally in 3.1.2 automatic identification of
some potions was added.

Forward to 3.4.3, other potions got auto-id at some version but not
the potion of hallucination.

> Engraving with wands reveals type of some of these.  Seems if the
> message given is obvious stick gets identified.  I do not remember
> NetHack asking to name the wand in any case.  Possibly because it
> still is there and I can name it if I wish to?

Yes, I guess that's the reason. Again different forks add some auto-id
to this for the ones with unique messages.

> Throwing gems at unicorns never ask you to name them.  I find it useful
> to name your stuff first.

That's is the recommended standard procedure AFAIK.

> The gems do not really get lost so this may
> be the reason NetHack does not ask you to call the gemstone type but
> I am skeptical about that.  Coaligned unicorn is rarely on my "wanted
> dead" list.  Anyone knows better reason?

My guess would be technical as the gem *might* get lost and without
object no docall'ing.

> Breaking unknown wand is hardly a wise thing to do but it can identify
> some wand types.  However, despite the stick being gone after your
> action you are not prompted to name it.  My guess is player can call
> the wand at his leisure before breaking it (plus, you need to confirm
> that action) so no point in querying for name.

It's also possible nobody thought about that as it is often an
emergency action and therefore there is no code for docall'ing.
Contrary to popular believe the DevTeam doesn't think of everything
(yeah, I'm a heretic).

> So, the rules would appear to be:
>  - If the item is not lost, do not ask to name.  Player can do it.
>  - If the object is lost in controlled conditions do not ask either.
>  - If there is a message on use that distinguishes the object but it
>    is not obvious, ask for a name anyway.
>  - If a message given can be generated by more than one item type
>    ask the player to name it.
>
> Is that correct?  Can you add to this?

If the object got deleted from memory you can't do a dcall on it
anymore. But without source diving you can't say in general if this is
the case or if there was just no dcall implemented.

> Another question has me wondering: does NetHack ever ask you to name
> an item solely because the message given is very similar to another
> one just to make player pay attention?

I wouldn't know an instance of this.

